think The README's own tail names the next crux: after lotus-audio (attack/release compressor) "the family's next rung can give the limiter and the gate the same attack and release." the Long Return Lila picks the gate — lotus-audio's exact mirror, the threshold inverted, so it reuses the two proven parents (lotus-audio gate, lotus-audio follower) with no new arithmetic on the audio path.
think One-clock: the wall clock read 174255-174311, behind the last seated artifact (lotus-audio at 174500). Monotonicity (later is always larger) wins — stamped lotus-audio at 174600, strictly ahead.
think The one case the compressor never faced: lotus-audio's branch was e > threshold (>= 1), so the divide by e was always safe. The gate's branch is e < threshold, and e can reach 0 at rest. Guarded exactly by env_step's forced-unit-step law — a zero envelope can only arise when the sample's own magnitude is zero — so no divide by zero, proven not clamped.
think Wrote lotus/gate_env.rye (gate_follow / gate_follow_carry), tools/ales_gate_env_witness.rish, and the design doc. Mirrored compress_env.rye's shape and its selftest, inverting the threshold and swapping the time-behavior test from "slower attack lets the transient through" to "slower release holds the gate open longer" (the gate's characteristic breath).
obs Byte-for-byte unit identity holds cleanly: at instant attack/release e = |x| = m, and divTrunc(m·divTrunc(m·den,num), m) = divTrunc(m·den,num) because m divides m·K exactly — the plain gate's own output.
